{"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","title":"Error Presentation","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}]},"abstract":[{"text":"Everything the default Error placeholders do beyond showing a generic icon and message: telling a connectivity problem apart from everything else, an optional second action alongside Retry, and a user-facing message distinct from ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"localizedDescription"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":".error"},{"type":"text","text":" is the one state whose presentation can’t be fully generic — a timed-out request, a missing article, and a malformed response all deserve different words, and sometimes a different action, even though they’re all just an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Error"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":". This article rounds up the three pieces "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" use to narrow that gap without you writing a fully custom placeholder."}]},{"level":2,"text":"The offline-aware variant","type":"heading","anchor":"The-offline-aware-variant"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/isConnectivityError(_: